-- title:
--   Frobenius acts as p Tₚ on toric torsion
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $N_0$ and $p$ with $N_0 \neq 0$ and $p$ prime, and suppose $p \nmid N_0$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbf{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $p$, in the sense that $p$ belongs to the non-units of $A$; let $\Lambda$ be a `LevelData` for $(N_0, p, A)$ — a scheme $X$ over the base of the $p$-adic situation together with a relative group law, a parametrisation of $\mathrm{JZero}\ N_0$ by generic-fibre points and of the special-fibre points over the residue field of $A$ — satisfying the predicate `IsJacobian` (abelian-scheme bundle, commutativity of the group law, additivity and Galois equivariance of the two parametrisations, compatibility of reduction of points, and realisability of every element of the Hecke algebra by an endomorphism over the base); and let $O$ be a `JZeroNeronObjectAtP` for these data. Assume further that for every $m$ coprime to $p$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbf{Q}$ (viewed inside $\mathrm{Gal}$ via the decomposition subgroup), and every $m$-torsion element $x$ of $\mathrm{JZero}(N_0p)$, the difference $\sigma \cdot x - x$ lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\ m$, the subgroup generated by the range of $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\ m$. Then, granting the level-$N_0p$ Hecke inputs `HeckeInputsAll (N₀ * p)` and the commutation of the Hecke operators `HeckeOperatorsCommuteBar (N₀ * p)`: for every $m > 0$ coprime to $p$, every $\mathbf{Q}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbf{Q}}$ that is a Frobenius at $A$ for $p$ (that is, $\sigma$ lies in the decomposition subgroup and acts on the residue field of $A$ by $y \mapsto y^p$), and every $x \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}\ m$, one has $\sigma \cdot x = (p \cdot T_p) \cdot x$, where $T_p$ is the generator `heckeGen ⟨p, _⟩` of `HeckeAlg = MvPolynomial Nat.Primes ℤ` and the action is the one supplied by `heckeModuleBar (N₀ * p)`.
--
--   This is the Néron-object form of the relation $\mathrm{Frob} = p\,U_p$ on the toric part of the prime-to-$p$ torsion of $J_0(N_0p)$ at a place above $p$, as in Ribet's analysis of the character group of the toric part via the dual graph. It feeds the construction of the ordinary-case data used in level lowering at $p$.
